  "verdict": {
    "outcome": "O2",
    "direction": "mixed",
    "magnitude": "moderate",
    "time_horizon": "this-parliament",
    "confidence": "moderate",
    "plain": "Requiring landlords to reach EPC C by 2028 should cut tenants' energy bills \u2014 especially for the poorest renters in the least efficient homes \u2014 but upgrade costs may be passed on through higher rents or reduced rental supply, which would push up housing costs. The net effect on tenants' overall cost of living depends heavily on whether landlords comply, exit, or pass costs on.",
    "biggest_unknown": "Whether landlords \u2014 especially small, single-property landlords with older homes \u2014 absorb upgrade costs, pass them on as higher rents, or exit the market, which would determine whether tenants see a net gain or loss in affordability.",
    "claims": [
      {
        "tier": "stated",
        "claim": "The policy requires landlords to upgrade properties to EPC C or above by 2028.",
        "evidence_id": "M",
        "quote": "Reintroduce requirements for landlords to upgrade the energy efficiency of their properties to EPC C or above by 2028."
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "Around 58% of private rented sector properties already meet EPC C, meaning roughly 42% do not and would require upgrades.",
        "evidence_id": "E32",
        "quote": "around 58% of private rented sector properties already meet the EPC C standard"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "Over 70% of low-income private renters live in homes with poor energy efficiency, meaning the policy targets those most in need.",
        "evidence_id": "E28",
        "quote": "over 70% of low-income private renters live in homes with poor energy efficiency"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "A home rated EPC E costs on average \u00a3595 more per year to heat than an EPC C home; an F/G-rated home costs \u00a31,339 more per year.",
        "evidence_id": "E14",
        "quote": "a home with an EPC band E costs, on average, \u00a3595 more per year to heat than one rated EPC C, and a home in band F/G costs \u00a31,339 per year more"
      },
      {
        "tier": "projected",
        "claim": "The policy is expected to lower energy bills and improve living conditions for tenants, with the greatest savings for vulnerable tenants.",
        "evidence_id": "E12",
        "quote": "Tenants are expected to benefit from lower energy bills and improved living conditions, including warmer homes less susceptible to damp and mould"
      },
      {
        "tier": "projected",
        "claim": "There are concerns that some landlords may sell up rather than invest in upgrades, potentially reducing rental supply and pushing up rents.",
        "evidence_id": "E16",
        "quote": "some landlords, particularly those with older, harder-to-treat properties, may choose to sell up rather than invest in costly upgrades, potentially reducing the supply of available rental properties and increasing rents"
      },
      {
        "tier": "projected",
        "claim": "Some contend that tenants indirectly bear the costs of landlord upgrades.",
        "evidence_id": "E40",
        "quote": "others contend that tenants indirectly bear these costs"
      }
    ],
    "rationale": "The policy directly targets the cost of keeping homes warm \u2014 the most acute energy cost for renters. The measurable baseline is stark: low-income renters are disproportionately concentrated in the least efficient homes, and the annual heating premium for a band-E or worse property runs to \u00a3595\u2013\u00a31,339. If landlords comply and do not pass costs on, tenants \u2014 especially the most vulnerable \u2014 stand to gain hundreds of pounds a year in lower energy bills, a meaningful improvement to cost of living.\n\nHowever, the cost burden on landlords is substantial: the Resolution Foundation puts the typical upgrade at ~\u00a39,000, rising to \u00a310,000\u2013\u00a325,000 for older solid-wall properties. Landlords facing these costs have three options: absorb them (improving tenant outcomes), pass them through as higher rents (partially or fully offsetting energy savings), or exit the market (reducing supply and potentially raising rents across the sector). Evidence on which dominates is genuinely split: the Resolution Foundation sees large-scale exits as unlikely given sector stability since 2015; landlord associations and some MPs warn of significant sell-offs, especially for small landlords with older stock.\n\nThe net O2 effect therefore depends on this crux. On energy bills alone, the direction is clearly positive for the ~42% of private renters in sub-C properties, with the poorest renters benefiting most. But if upgrade costs feed through to rents \u2014 even partially \u2014 the disposable-income gain is eroded. The exemption cap (spend up to a limit, then register for exemption) limits worst-case landlord losses but also means the hardest-to-treat properties may never be upgraded, capping the benefit for tenants in those homes. On balance, the bill-reduction benefit is real and evidence-backed; the rent-pass-through risk is also real but disputed. Both effects are material enough to call this mixed rather than a clear improvement.",
